# meta::salientEntities = 12 (5*), 14 (5*), 145 (5*), 3 (4*), 7 (3*), 16 (3*), 23 (3), 81 (3), 95 (3*), 2 (2), 6 (2), 21 (2*), 31 (2), 97 (2*), 4 (1), 10 (1), 18 (1), 73 (1), 94 (1*), 99 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://www.reddit.com/r/explainlikeimfive/comments/6zm74h/eli5_if_every_major_country_is_in_billions_if_not/
# meta::speakerCount = 2
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In a post answering the question how and to whom countries can be in debt, the author explains how money is a form of debt whose value depends on the amount of money in circulation, and how fiat currency developed as a substitute for gold reserves which formerly backed bank debt certificates.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) A user from an online forum is explaining to the author of the post that governments in large amounts of debt borrow money from its citizens through means like certificates, and that this system works because these governments use a fiat currency, rather than a gold backed currency.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) In answer to a question about where countries can borrow from if every major country is in debt, the author of the post responds by writing about the role of money and its differences from fiat currency, explaining that US currency has value only because the US government says it does and that printing more money deteriorates its value, before apologizing for rambling.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) One user asks where countries borrow money from if they are billions of dollars in debt, and one user shared that countries can borrow money from their people (a printed note represents a debt owed the bearer), that printing more money can devalue currency, and that money’s value is determined by what people believe has value, such as the U.S. government’s security or gold.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) A reddit user responds to a post about countries in debt borrowing money explaining that printing more money devalues money in circulation, fiat currency was developed such that money only has value because the government says it has value, and the government borrows from its people so in theory, the government owes its population money.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) In response to the question of where countries borrow money from if they are all in debt a forum user explains that countries borrow money from their people and print paper money as proof, and that this money only has value because people believe it does and trust that the government is good for it, so they cannot print a large amount of money without devaluing their currency.
